{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The sacral plexus is a network of nerves in the pelvis, lying on the front of piriformis. It is formed by the ventral rami of L4 to S3 and part of S4. Its two main branches are the sciatic and pudendal nerves. Other branches supply the gluteal muscles, the skin of the buttock and back of the thigh, and the pelvic floor.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN48.4" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## What is the sacral plexus?

A **plexus** is a network in which the ventral rami of spinal nerves join, split and rejoin before giving off the nerves that go to the body. A **ventral ramus** is the front branch of a spinal nerve.

The sacral plexus is part of the **lumbosacral plexus**. The nerves of the pelvis include the lumbosacral plexus, the coccygeal plexus and the pelvic autonomic nerves.

The L4 to S4 roots supply the sacral plexus. It supplies all the muscles below the knee and the muscles of the lateral and posterior thigh. Its sensory supply covers the posterior thigh, and the shin, calf and foot, except the medial shin and foot. That area is supplied by the saphenous nerve, which comes from the lumbar plexus.

## Formation

The plexus is formed by the ventral rami of **part of L4, the whole of L5, S1, S2, S3** and part of S4.

- The **lumbosacral trunk** is made from the descending branch of the ventral ramus of L4 and the whole of the ventral ramus of L5. It runs down over the ala of the sacrum, crosses the pelvic brim in front of the sacroiliac joint, and joins S1.
- S1 and S2 are large. The lower sacral nerves become progressively smaller.
- Each ventral ramus receives a **grey ramus communicans** from the sympathetic chain at the anterior sacral foramen. There are no white rami communicantes at this level.
- Before they unite, the ventral rami give off twigs to piriformis (S1, S2), twigs to levator ani, coccygeus and the external anal sphincter (S4), and the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2, S3, S4).

## Position and relations

- The main plexus lies **in front of piriformis**, deep to the pelvic fascia. It is behind the internal iliac vessels and the ureter.
- The lumbosacral trunk and S1 are separated by the superior gluteal vessels. S1 and S2 are separated by the inferior gluteal vessels.
- S2, S3 and part of S4 lie between the front of piriformis and the pelvic fascia.
- The lumbosacral trunk and S1 lie in front of the sacroiliac joint, so disease of that joint can involve them.

## Divisions and the general rule

As in other plexuses, the rami divide into **ventral divisions** and **dorsal divisions**. In general:

- Dorsal divisions supply the **extensors and abductors** of the limb.
- Ventral divisions supply the **flexors and adductors**.

The plexus gives two main branches: the **sciatic nerve**, concerned with locomotion, and the **pudendal nerve**, concerned with reproduction.

## Branches of the sacral plexus (AN48.4)

{% table %}
- **Nerve**
- **Roots and division**
- **Supplies**
---
- **Nerve to quadratus femoris**
- L4, L5, S1 (ventral)
- Quadratus femoris, inferior gemellus and the hip joint
---
- **Nerve to obturator internus**
- L5, S1, S2 (ventral)
- Obturator internus and superior gemellus
---
- **Nerve to piriformis**
- S1, S2 (dorsal)
- Piriformis only
---
- **Superior gluteal nerve**
- L4, L5, S1 (dorsal)
- Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae
---
- **Inferior gluteal nerve**
- L5, S1, S2 (dorsal)
- Gluteus maximus
---
- **Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve (posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh)**
- S2, S3 (ventral) and S1, S2 (dorsal)
- Skin of the gluteal region, perineum, back of the thigh and upper back of the leg
---
- **Perforating cutaneous nerve**
- S2, S3 (dorsal)
- Skin of the lower medial part of the buttock
---
- **Tibial part of the sciatic nerve**
- L4, L5, S1, S2, S3 (ventral)
- Hamstrings, all calf muscles and the muscles of the sole
---
- **Common fibular part of the sciatic nerve**
- L4, L5, S1, S2 (dorsal)
- Evertors of the foot, dorsiflexors of the ankle and extensor digitorum brevis
---
- **Pudendal nerve**
- S2, S3, S4 (ventral)
- Perineal muscles, external genital organs and skin
---
- **Branches to levator ani, coccygeus and external anal sphincter**
- S4 (ventral)
- Pelvic floor muscles and upper external anal sphincter
---
- **Pelvic splanchnic nerves**
- S2, S3, S4
- Pelvic viscera (parasympathetic)
{% /table %}

Some sources give slightly different root values for a few small branches, so check which version your department uses.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Dorsal or ventral?** Think of the limb: dorsal divisions go to extensors and abductors, ventral divisions go to flexors and adductors. The common fibular part (dorsiflexors, evertors) is dorsal. The tibial part (hamstrings, calf, sole) is ventral.
{% /callout %}

## Leaving the pelvis

Most branches to the lower limb leave through the **greater sciatic foramen**. The sciatic, inferior gluteal, posterior femoral cutaneous and the nerves to obturator internus and quadratus femoris leave **below piriformis**. The superior gluteal nerve lies along the upper border of piriformis.

The pudendal nerve also leaves through the greater sciatic foramen, but it enters the perineum through the **lesser sciatic foramen**.

## Sciatic nerve

- It is the **largest nerve in the body** and the largest branch of the plexus. It is about 2 cm wide at its origin.
- It consists of the **tibial and common fibular nerves** bound together by fascia. Root value is L4, L5, S1, S2, S3.
- It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis. It then runs down the buttock under cover of gluteus maximus, lying on the ischial spine area, then on the superior gemellus, obturator internus, inferior gemellus and quadratus femoris. It reaches the back of adductor magnus and passes deep to the long head of biceps femoris into the thigh.
- It usually gives **no branches in the gluteal region**.
- In the thigh it divides into the tibial and common fibular nerves at a variable level above the popliteal fossa.
- It gives articular branches to the hip joint and muscular branches to biceps femoris, semitendinosus, semimembranosus and the ischial part of adductor magnus.

**Surface marking:** in the buttock it runs from a point midway between the posterosuperior iliac spine and the ischial tuberosity, then midway between the tip of the greater trochanter and the ischial tuberosity. In the thigh it runs down the midline of the back.

**Relation to piriformis is variable.** The undivided nerve may emerge above or through the muscle. The common variant is that one division passes between the heads of a divided muscle and the other above or below. Piriformis can occasionally entrap the sciatic nerve.

**What it supplies:** the hamstrings (all from the tibial division except the short head of biceps femoris, which comes from the common fibular). Together the tibial and common fibular nerves supply all motor function below the knee. The common fibular nerve supplies the lateral and anterior compartments (ankle dorsiflexion and eversion, toe extensors). The tibial nerve supplies the posterior compartment and the foot muscles (plantar flexion, inversion, toe flexors).

## Pudendal nerve

The pudendal nerve (S2, S3, S4) supplies the skin, external genital organs and muscles of the perineum. It is concerned with micturition, defaecation, erection, ejaculation and, in females, childbirth.

**Course in three parts:**

1. **In the pelvis:** it forms just above the sacrotuberous ligament and descends in front of piriformis. It leaves through the greater sciatic foramen between piriformis and coccygeus, medial to the internal pudendal vessels.
2. **In the gluteal region:** it crosses the sacrospinous ligament close to the ischial spine, under cover of gluteus maximus. It lies medial to the internal pudendal vessels. It then passes through the lesser sciatic foramen.
3. **In the pudendal (Alcock's) canal:** this lies on the lateral wall of the ischio-anal fossa. In the posterior part of the canal it gives the **inferior rectal nerve**, then divides into the **perineal nerve** and the **dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris**.

The inferior rectal nerve crosses the ischio-anal fossa and supplies the external anal sphincter, the skin around the anus and the lining of the anal canal below the pectinate line.

Two clinical points:

- The **ischial spine** is the landmark when injecting local anaesthetic for a pudendal nerve block.
- The nerve is relatively fixed behind the sacrospinous ligament and in the canal, so it may be stretched when the pelvic floor is pushed down in childbirth.

## Other branches in brief

- **Pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2 to S4):** the sacral part of the parasympathetic system. They carry preganglionic parasympathetic fibres to small ganglia in the walls of the pelvic viscera.
- **Perforating cutaneous nerve:** pierces the sacrotuberous ligament, curves round the lower border of gluteus maximus and supplies the skin over its inferomedial part. It may arise from the pudendal nerve or be absent.
- **Posterior femoral cutaneous nerve:** leaves below piriformis and runs down under gluteus maximus, behind or medial to the sciatic nerve. It runs in the back of the thigh deep to the fascia lata and pierces the deep fascia behind the knee. Its gluteal branches (inferior clunial nerves) curl round the lower border of gluteus maximus.
- **Sympathetic fibres:** postganglionic fibres reach the sacral and coccygeal spinal nerves through grey rami. They travel in the plexus branches, for example in the tibial nerve to the popliteal artery and its branches.

## Coccygeal plexus

This is a small plexus formed by the ventral rami of S4 (descending branch), S5 and the coccygeal nerve. The three nerves join on the pelvic surface of coccygeus. The plexus gives off the **anococcygeal nerves**, which pierce the sacrotuberous ligament and supply the skin near the coccyx.

## Dermatomes of the sacral region

A **dermatome** is the area of skin supplied by one spinal nerve root. The S1 dermatome includes the little toe. The S3, S4 and S5 dermatomes form concentric rings around the anus. The L5 dermatome runs diagonally from the outer side of the tibia to the inner side of the foot.

## Clinical anatomy

**Why lesions of the plexus are uncommon:** the plexus lies deep and protected. When it is affected, the usual causes are pelvic malignancy (a carcinoma of the rectum can cause severe pain down the lower limbs), fractures of the pelvis, severe pelvic haemorrhage, and temporary lesions after pregnancy and childbirth, such as a difficult forceps delivery of a large baby. Pain, often diffuse, is the most common feature. Late in pregnancy, pressure from the fetal head can cause an aching pain down one lower limb, often relieved by changing position.

**Sacroiliac joint disease:** the lumbosacral trunk and S1 lie in front of the joint, so disease there can cause pain below the knee.

**Sciatica:** pain in the lower back and hip that radiates down the posterior thigh to the leg. It is often caused by a herniated intervertebral disc compressing the L5 or S1 ventral rami. The so-called **piriformis syndrome**, from an anomalous relationship between piriformis and the sciatic nerve, can give similar pain.

**Sciatic nerve injury:**

- Causes: penetrating wounds, pelvic fractures, hip dislocations, and most often **badly placed intramuscular injections** in the gluteal region.
- To avoid injury, give gluteal injections in the **upper outer quadrant** of the buttock. The lower medial quadrant contains the sciatic nerve and must be avoided.
- Most lesions are incomplete. In **90% of injuries the common fibular part is most affected**, probably because its fibres lie most superficially in the nerve.
- The hamstrings are paralysed, but weak knee flexion remains because of sartorius (femoral nerve) and gracilis (obturator nerve).
- Common fibular involvement gives **foot drop** and a **high-stepping gait**.

**Superior gluteal nerve:** it supplies gluteus medius and minimus. These muscles may be paralysed when poliomyelitis involves the lower lumbar and sacral spinal cord segments. The patient then has serious difficulty tilting the pelvis when walking.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1, S2) is the nerve to gluteus maximus. The superior gluteal nerve supplies gluteus medius, gluteus minimus and tensor fasciae latae." %}
Gluteus maximus is supplied by the:
- A. Superior gluteal nerve
- B. Inferior gluteal nerve
- C. Nerve to piriformis
- D. Sciatic nerve
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below piriformis, and then descends in the buttock under gluteus maximus." %}
The sciatic nerve leaves the pelvis:
- A. Through the lesser sciatic foramen
- B. Above piriformis through the greater sciatic foramen
- C. Below piriformis through the greater sciatic foramen
- D. Through the obturator canal
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="The pudendal nerve arises from the ventral divisions of S2, S3 and S4 and is concerned with micturition, defaecation, erection, ejaculation and childbirth." %}
The root value of the pudendal nerve is:
- A. L4, L5, S1
- B. L5, S1, S2
- C. S1, S2, S3
- D. S2, S3, S4
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The upper outer quadrant is the safe area. The lower and medial quadrant contains the sciatic nerve, and badly placed injections are the most frequent cause of its injury." %}
Which site is safest for an intramuscular injection in the buttock?
- A. Upper outer quadrant
- B. Lower outer quadrant
- C. Upper inner quadrant
- D. Lower inner quadrant
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="In about 90% of sciatic nerve injuries the common fibular part is most affected, giving foot drop and a high-stepping gait. The common fibular fibres probably lie most superficially in the nerve." %}
In an incomplete sciatic nerve injury, which part is most often affected, and what is the typical result?
- A. Tibial part, with loss of knee extension
- B. Common fibular part, with foot drop
- C. Tibial part, with foot drop
- D. Common fibular part, with loss of hip adduction
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1) arises from the dorsal divisions. Dorsal divisions supply extensors and abductors. The tibial nerve, pudendal nerve and nerve to obturator internus arise from ventral divisions." %}
Which branch of the sacral plexus arises from the dorsal divisions?
- A. Tibial part of the sciatic nerve
- B. Pudendal nerve
- C. Superior gluteal nerve
- D. Nerve to obturator internus
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Which roots form the sacral plexus?" %}
Ventral rami of part of L4, the whole of L5, S1, S2, S3 and part of S4. L4 and L5 reach it as the lumbosacral trunk, which joins S1.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Where does the sacral plexus lie?" %}
In the pelvis, on the front of piriformis, deep to the pelvic fascia. It is behind the internal iliac vessels and the ureter.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the two main branches of the plexus." %}
The sciatic nerve, concerned with locomotion, and the pudendal nerve, concerned with reproduction.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the sciatic nerve made of and what is its surface marking?" %}
The tibial and common fibular nerves bound together by fascia. In the buttock it runs from midway between the posterosuperior iliac spine and ischial tuberosity, to midway between the greater trochanter and ischial tuberosity, then down the midline of the back of the thigh.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the course of the pudendal nerve." %}
It leaves the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen between piriformis and coccygeus, crosses the sacrospinous ligament near the ischial spine, re-enters through the lesser sciatic foramen and runs in the pudendal canal on the lateral wall of the ischio-anal fossa, where it gives the inferior rectal and perineal nerves and the dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is the landmark for a pudendal nerve block?" %}
The ischial spine.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How can the sciatic nerve be injured in the buttock, and how is this avoided?" %}
Most often by a badly placed intramuscular injection. Injections should be given in the upper outer quadrant of the buttock.
{% /viva %}

